  "overview": "The Arduino Nano packs the same ATmega328P brain as the Uno into a breadboard-friendly board about a third of the size, with male pin headers instead of the Uno's female sockets so it can be plugged straight into a breadboard. It trades the Uno's barrel jack and full-size USB-B for a compact USB connector (Mini-USB on older boards, Micro-USB on the Nano v3.x, USB-C on newer clones) and gains two extra analog-only pins (A6/A7). It's the board of choice whenever a project needs Uno-level capability but Uno-sized real estate isn't available.",
  "simpleSummary": "A shrunk-down Arduino Uno that plugs straight into a breadboard.",
